  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A couple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \nfor late week may produce a strong storm or two. Rainfall will \nbe limited to where convection forms, allowing for some \nworsening of ongoing dry conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nOn Thursday the large 500mb high over the Deep South begins to\npush a large ridge over the Central US. At the same time a trof\nbegins to dig south over the Great Lakes and Eastern Canada. A \nsurface warm front will be lifting thru the CWA during the day.\nShowers move in from the west in the afternoon with \nthunderstorms possible during the afternoon SBCAPE between \n500-1000 J/kg, 0-6km shear >30kts, strong low level lapse rates\nand modest mid level lapse rates. This may bring strong storms \nto the area with decent dynamics working into the area but\nconfidence is low at this point.\n\nGFS/ECMWF/Canadian models differ significantly on timing of the\ntrof and cold front but expecting showers into the night. The \nupper level trof remains progged to move thru during the day on\nFriday, along with the cold pool. This will result in another\nround of diurnally driven storms. PWATs are pretty meager, \nranging from 1-1.50 inches, highest over Downeast so not seeing \nthe potential for heavy rain at this point. \n\nConvection will result in limited precipitation in most \nlocations which will continue to worsening the ongoing \ndry/drought conditions. D0-D1 conditions are present in southern\nand eastern areas of the CWA. Much of the streamflows across the\nCWA are Below to Much Below Normal for this time of year along\nwith Groundwater wells rapidly falling to below normal to low\nend normal conditions. Lack of significant widespread rainfall\nwill result in worsening conditions in terms of the drought.",
